WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.510
Of course, we want to speak the
language eventually and we want to

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:09.570
speak well, however, there's no need to
be anxious about how we're doing now.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.400
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:16.079
Today I want to talk about something
that I've been thinking about recently,

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:21.420
and that is the importance of not
speaking the language we're learning.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:25.050
Don't speak the language we're,
learning a little bit provocative

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.370
and I'll explain what I mean.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.280
Meanwhile, if you enjoy these
videos, please subscribe.

00:00:30.165 --> 00:00:32.085
Uh, I click on that
bell for notifications.

00:00:32.085 --> 00:00:36.525
If you are listening to this on a
podcast service, please leave a review.

00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:38.925
Um, I hope my sound is better.

00:00:39.555 --> 00:00:43.935
There have been some complaints
now,.What do I mean by don't speak?

00:00:44.055 --> 00:00:44.445
All right.

00:00:44.504 --> 00:00:46.635
I'll tell you my, my sort
of reasoning on this.

00:00:47.205 --> 00:00:51.285
Um, recently I decided that in my
Arabic, I was going to move over to

00:00:51.285 --> 00:00:55.485
Egyptian Arabic because I wanted to try
to understand Egyptian movies better.

00:00:55.905 --> 00:01:02.370
Egypt is a country of 90 or more million
people and they have a language with

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:08.670
its own culture, humor, expressions,
uh, which I can connect to in some way.

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.220
Uh, that's not true of standard Arabic.

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:15.000
There is no country where the
local language is standard Arabic.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.420
And so it's a little bit
of an artificial language.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:20.220
It's like Latin or something.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:23.670
Not quite, it is spoken by many Arabs.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.429
It's spoken in official
situations by politicians.

00:01:27.404 --> 00:01:32.294
Uh, the written material on the
internet is mostly in standard Arabic,

00:01:32.774 --> 00:01:36.494
but in terms of getting that sort of
resonance that you get from a real

00:01:36.494 --> 00:01:39.554
life country, it doesn't have that.

00:01:39.945 --> 00:01:45.824
So I started doing, um, you know,
Egyptian Arabic and I found that a couple

00:01:45.824 --> 00:01:47.324
of things that were very interesting.

00:01:47.354 --> 00:01:52.725
One, my Arabic is now ruined, my
standard Arabic, my Egyptian Arabic.

00:01:52.725 --> 00:01:54.884
I have trouble saying anything.

00:01:54.884 --> 00:01:55.695
I struggle to say it.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.920
On the other hand, my spoken,
uh, Persian, Farsi has improved

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.960
without me putting anything into it.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:08.130
All of a sudden I have my online
discussions with Sahra and, and she

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.930
agrees I'm doing better than ever.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.980
So it's like I left my Persian alone.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.765
I still have, you know, twice a week
sessions with Sahra, uh, but leaving

00:02:18.765 --> 00:02:24.345
Persian alone and even leaving Standard
Arabic and stimulating my brain with this

00:02:24.345 --> 00:02:30.015
exposure to Egyptian Arabic, with all of
its idiosyncrasies seems to have improved.

00:02:30.015 --> 00:02:31.635
My Persian sounds a bit strange.

00:02:31.725 --> 00:02:37.515
I have experienced this before that if
you work very hard at language A then

00:02:37.515 --> 00:02:42.955
leave language A and go to language B,
come back to language A and you understand

00:02:42.975 --> 00:02:44.235
it better and you can speak better.

00:02:44.805 --> 00:02:45.465
Why that is?

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:46.065
I don't know.

00:02:46.065 --> 00:02:49.395
I have called that sort of
the period of benign neglect.

00:02:49.695 --> 00:02:51.675
It may be a function of leaving it.

00:02:51.705 --> 00:02:55.245
It may be a function of trying
very hard to learn something else

00:02:55.395 --> 00:02:57.615
which is stimulating for the brain.

00:02:57.645 --> 00:03:00.735
Then coming back to something familiar
and all of a sudden you're better

00:03:00.735 --> 00:03:03.135
able to recall words in that language.

00:03:03.195 --> 00:03:04.245
I don't know why.

00:03:04.815 --> 00:03:09.015
However, I had a session this
morning in Egyptian Arabic.

00:03:09.585 --> 00:03:12.435
I mean, I couldn't say anything,
not in Egyptian Arabic not in

00:03:12.435 --> 00:03:14.655
Standard Arabic, but I enjoy it.

00:03:15.674 --> 00:03:16.515
I enjoyed it.

00:03:16.515 --> 00:03:22.394
And I enjoy listening to the material
that I have in Egyptian Arabic.

00:03:22.424 --> 00:03:24.885
I enjoy discovering Egyptian Arabic.

00:03:25.364 --> 00:03:30.614
I'm looking forward to being better at
Egyptian Arabic, but I'm not concerned

00:03:30.704 --> 00:03:33.315
about my inability to speak right now.

00:03:33.915 --> 00:03:38.864
Um, I enjoy these sessions
with Adel Samy my new tutor in

00:03:39.195 --> 00:03:41.804
Alexandria, because he's funny.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.650
And he keeps me going and we have
almost philosophical discussions where

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:50.520
he has to give me the words and he
writes the words out on the screen

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:52.590
before I'm actually able to say them.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:53.700
But that's fine.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.360
I certainly couldn't carry
on a conversation, but

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:58.530
it's extremely enjoyable.

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:04.920
And so the sort of passive, you know,
discovering the language I find enjoyable.

00:04:05.685 --> 00:04:11.295
If I were genuinely concerned about
how weak I am in speaking the language,

00:04:11.325 --> 00:04:13.005
I would feel, you know, distraught.

00:04:13.424 --> 00:04:18.045
And that's why I kind of reminded
myself that, you know, very often

00:04:18.075 --> 00:04:21.855
people identify learning a language
with being able to speak the language.

00:04:21.855 --> 00:04:23.205
And what can I say now?

00:04:23.445 --> 00:04:27.284
And of course we want to speak the
language eventually and we want to

00:04:27.284 --> 00:04:32.775
speak well, however, there's no need
to be anxious about how we're doing

00:04:32.835 --> 00:04:37.365
now or at the beginning, or even a
fair way along in the language, as

00:04:37.365 --> 00:04:39.075
long as we are enjoying the language.

00:04:39.465 --> 00:04:44.055
And so, uh, I think very often in
the sort of standard classroom,

00:04:44.055 --> 00:04:47.355
there's a tremendous emphasis placed
on producing the language, speaking

00:04:47.355 --> 00:04:49.965
the language, which is fine because
that's what people want to do.

00:04:50.534 --> 00:04:55.145
But what I think is more important
is what will I be able to do with

00:04:55.145 --> 00:04:56.825
them, the language in a year from now?

00:04:57.365 --> 00:05:02.145
And from that perspective, I'm not
tremendously motivated to speak.

00:05:02.145 --> 00:05:06.724
Now I have this a couple of sessions
a week just to maintain that human

00:05:06.724 --> 00:05:10.565
contact with, with someone who
speaks the language, but it doesn't

00:05:10.565 --> 00:05:11.974
bother me that I can't speak well.

00:05:12.905 --> 00:05:16.505
No, I fully appreciate that there
are situations where we need to

00:05:16.505 --> 00:05:19.864
learn to speak the language because
we have immigrated to our country.

00:05:20.164 --> 00:05:23.705
And, and when you're in that situation,
of course, you're getting a lot

00:05:23.705 --> 00:05:26.945
of input, a lot of input you're
listening and reading all the time.

00:05:27.615 --> 00:05:29.565
And you're trying to
speak wherever you can.

00:05:29.565 --> 00:05:34.245
And so you have this urgency to speak,
which is fine, but even there, you

00:05:34.245 --> 00:05:39.255
shouldn't be concerned about short-term
difficulties in using the language

00:05:39.525 --> 00:05:41.235
because it's been my experience.

00:05:41.325 --> 00:05:44.175
I mean, I can remember when I was
learning Czech, for example, I was six

00:05:44.175 --> 00:05:48.795
months at, at listening to stuff on Czech
history, fascinating stuff about the the

00:05:48.795 --> 00:05:54.755
Austro-Hungarian  empire or Prague, and,
uh, you know, under, um, Charles you know,

00:05:54.755 --> 00:05:56.735
king Charles back in the 14th century.

00:05:57.795 --> 00:06:00.975
And I, I went to this body shop
where the person there spoke Czech.

00:06:00.975 --> 00:06:01.845
And I couldn't say a thing.

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:06.195
I couldn't say a thing, but now,
and when I went to, first of all,

00:06:06.195 --> 00:06:08.425
now I can understand so much Czech.

00:06:08.445 --> 00:06:11.235
I can understand, you know,
what people say and little

00:06:11.235 --> 00:06:12.735
clips that I find on Twitter.

00:06:13.275 --> 00:06:16.725
Uh, and when I was in the Czech
Republic, I was communicating just fine.

00:06:17.355 --> 00:06:22.455
So the point is, again, I guess it's
a common theme that I have is don't

00:06:22.455 --> 00:06:23.805
put too much pressure on yourself.

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:28.860
Uh, obviously, if you're in a situation
where you, you need to speak better

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:35.120
quickly, even there fastest path to
that is lots of input activities.

00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:39.350
And speaking wherever you have the
chance, but if you are on a, as I

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:42.290
am in the case of Arabic, I have
no immediate urgency to speak the

00:06:42.290 --> 00:06:44.510
language I achieve what I achieve.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:45.350
Twice a week.

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.660
I speak sometimes better, sometimes worse.

00:06:47.870 --> 00:06:49.250
I focus on input.

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:50.540
I'm enjoying it.

00:06:51.710 --> 00:06:52.040
Good enough.

00:06:52.965 --> 00:06:55.005
And there could be some surprises.

00:06:55.034 --> 00:06:59.234
Maybe if I move on to Turkish and
then I come back to Arabic, all of

00:06:59.234 --> 00:07:03.104
a sudden my Arabic speaking ability
will improve as was the case with

00:07:03.104 --> 00:07:08.085
my Persian speaking ability after
this period of benign neglect.

00:07:08.445 --> 00:07:10.455
So don't be too fast.

00:07:10.485 --> 00:07:15.195
I shouldn't say don't speak, but it
needn't be, you know, the objective number

00:07:15.195 --> 00:07:19.455
one in the early stages of learning the
language, it's more a matter of what

00:07:19.455 --> 00:07:21.555
you feel like doing and what your needs.

00:07:22.305 --> 00:07:23.685
So thank you for listening.

00:07:23.745 --> 00:07:24.045
Bye for now.

